/*
# pointertransparent — the chrome-overlay routing probe

`View.pointerEvents = "none"` is documented for "a full-viewport chrome overlay
— so presses reach what is beneath it". An overlay that can hold nothing
interactive is a highlight, not chrome, so the rule has to say what happens to
what is INSIDE the transparent view as well as what is under it.

The answer, which all three renderers now give: **"none" makes THAT VIEW
transparent and seals nothing.** Each descendant answers for itself — which is
what the DOM does, because dom-backend gives any view carrying a sink
`pointer-events: auto`, and an explicit value beats an inherited one.

Nothing in the corpus asked this until 2026-08-17, and the three hit walks had
quietly disagreed for as long as they had existed: the model walk, the canvas
backend and the mac backend all returned null at a "none" and never looked
inside, so on two of three renderers an overlay's own panel was unreachable.
Measured, before: DOM 1/101, canvas 0/0, mac 0/0.

Every layer here is stacked at the same depth on purpose, so a backend that
seals the subtree gives a DIFFERENT ANSWER — `app` or nothing — rather than a
less precise one.
*/

App [ appName = "Pointer Transparent", pointerEvents = "none",
    width = 600, height = 300, fill = #FFFFFF,

    // states its own "auto": the overlay's interactive panel (the Inspector's
    // own window is exactly this view)

    panel: View [ pointerEvents = "auto", x = 60, y = 60, width = 200, height = 120,
        fill = 0x2E9E4F,
        onClick() { }
        ],

    // states nothing, but CARRIES A SINK: the DOM realizes that as
    // `pointer-events: auto`, so it takes the point too

    sink: View [ x = 300, y = 60, width = 200, height = 120,
        fill = 0x3366CC,
        onClick() { }
        ],

    // states nothing and carries NO sink — pure decoration, and the one thing
    // in here that must never take a press

    decor: View [ x = 60, y = 200, width = 200, height = 60, fill = 0xCCCCCC ]
    ]
